Notifier Overview
-----------------------

JRebirth has got its own Notification Engine to allow each component to communicate with each other.
All these communications are processed into a dedicated thread described [before](Thread.html).
The notification engine uses custom objects to transport data: Waves.

The **Notifier** is JRebirth's internal event bus, running on JIT (JRebirth Internal Thread).
It dispatches **Wave** objects to any component that has registered interest in a particular **WaveType**.
This decoupled messaging system ensures that Models, Services, and Commands can interact without holding direct references to one another.

The typical flow is:

1. A component **registers** (listens) for one or more WaveTypes.
2. Another component **sends** a Wave carrying a WaveType and optional data.
3. The Notifier finds every component that listens for that WaveType and invokes the matching handler method.


Registering for Waves
-----------------------

To receive Waves, a component must call `listen(WaveType...)` during its initialization phase.
For a Model this is typically done inside `initModel()`, for a Service inside `initService()`, and for a Command inside `initCommand()`.

The `listen` method is defined in the `Component` interface:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.api.component.basic;

import java.lang.reflect.Method;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.FacadeReady;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.WaveBean;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.checker.WaveChecker;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveData;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveType;

/**
 * The interface <strong>EnhancedComponent</strong>.
 *
 * Define the contract used to manage waves.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 *
 * @param <R> A type that implements FacadeReady
 */
public interface Component<R extends FacadeReady<R>> extends FacadeReady<R> {

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param method the annotated method concerned
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final Method method, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Stop to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * @param waveTypes the type(s) to stop to listen
     */
    void unlisten(final WaveType... waveTypes);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param wave the wave to send
     */
    void sendWave(final Wave wave);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param waveType the type of wave to send
     * @param waveBean the wave bean
     *
    // ...
```

You can listen for several WaveTypes at once:

```java
@Override
protected void initModel() {
    listen(MyWaves.DO_ACTION_A, MyWaves.DO_ACTION_B);
}
```

- `initModel()` — correct hook for Models; Services use `initService()`, Commands use `initCommand()`.
- `listen(...)` — registers this component with the Notifier (strong reference until `unlisten`).
- Multiple `WaveType`s — one registration call for all events this component handles.

When `listen` is called, the component registers itself with the Notifier for the given WaveType(s).
The Notifier holds a **strong reference** to the component, so it will not be garbage-collected as long as it remains registered.

Here is a real-world example from the Analyzer showcase:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.ui.controls;

import java.util.List;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.JRebirthEvent;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.core.ui.DefaultModel;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.service.LoadEdtFileService;
import org.jrebirth.af.showcase.analyzer.ui.editor.EditorWaves;

/**
 * The class <strong>ControlsModel</strong>.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 */
public final class ControlsModel extends DefaultModel<ControlsModel, ControlsView> {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    protected void initModel() {
        listen(LoadEdtFileService.DO_LOAD_EVENTS.returnWaveType());
        listen(EditorWaves.DO_UNLOAD);
    }

    /**
     * Call when event are loaded.
     *
     * @param eventList the list of events loaded
     *
     * @param wave the wave received
     */
    public void doEventsLoaded(final List<JRebirthEvent> eventList, final Wave wave) {
        view().activateButtons(!eventList.isEmpty());
    }

    /**
     * Call when event are loaded.
     *
     * @param wave the wave received
     */
    public void doUnload(final Wave wave) {
        view().activateButtons(false);
    }
}
```

- `listen(...returnWaveType())` — service return waves use `.returnWaveType()` on the entry `WaveType`.
- `doEventsLoaded(List, Wave)` — name from return action `EVENTS_LOADED`; updates View via `view()`.
- `doUnload(Wave)` — parameterless action wave; only the trailing `Wave` argument is required.

The handler method name is derived from the WaveType action. For example, if the WaveType action is `DO_LOAD_EVENTS`, the handler method must be named `doEventsLoaded` (camelCase, matching the action string).


```java
    public void doEventsLoaded(final List<JRebirthEvent> eventList, final Wave wave) {
        view().activateButtons(!eventList.isEmpty());
    }
```


Unregistering
-----------------------

To stop receiving Waves, call `unlisten(WaveType...)`.
This removes the component from the Notifier and releases the strong reference, preventing potential memory leaks.


```java
    void unlisten(final WaveType... waveTypes);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param wave the wave to send
     */
    void sendWave(final Wave wave);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param waveType the type of wave to send
     * @param waveBean the wave bean
     *
     * @return the wave created and sent to JIT, be careful when you use a strong reference it can hold a lot of objects
     */
    <WB extends WaveBean> Wave sendWave(final WaveType waveType, final WB waveBean);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param waveType the type of wave to send
     * @param waveData the data (key-value
     *
     * @return the wave created and sent to JIT, be careful when you use a strong reference it can hold a lot of objects
     */
    Wave sendWave(final WaveType waveType, final WaveData<?>... waveData);

    /**
     * Return the root component (for inner component).
     *
     * @return the root component of the inner component
     */
    Component<?> rootComponent();

    /**
     * Define the root component for an inner component.
     *
     * @param rootComponent The root component to set.
     */
    void rootComponent(final Component<?> rootComponent);

    // /**
    // * Add an inner component.
    // *
    // * It will instantiate the component from the right Facade and store it into the current component.
    // *
    // * Shall be called from {@link AbstractComponent}.initInnerComponent (protected API)
```

```java
@Override
protected void unlistenOnDispose() {
    unlisten(MyWaves.DO_REFRESH);
}
```

- `unlisten(WaveType...)` — removes this component from the Notifier for those types.
- Models are auto-unlistened on release; call explicitly for long-lived components that change behavior.


Sending Waves
-----------------------

Any component can send a Wave via the `sendWave` methods defined on the `Component` interface:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.api.component.basic;

import java.lang.reflect.Method;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.FacadeReady;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.WaveBean;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.checker.WaveChecker;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveData;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveType;

/**
 * The interface <strong>EnhancedComponent</strong>.
 *
 * Define the contract used to manage waves.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 *
 * @param <R> A type that implements FacadeReady
 */
public interface Component<R extends FacadeReady<R>> extends FacadeReady<R> {

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param method the annotated method concerned
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final Method method, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Stop to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * @param waveTypes the type(s) to stop to listen
     */
    void unlisten(final WaveType... waveTypes);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param wave the wave to send
     */
    void sendWave(final Wave wave);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param waveType the type of wave to send
     * @param waveBean the wave bean
     *
    // ...
```

There are several ways to send a Wave:

**1. Using WaveType + WaveData**

```java
sendWave(MyWaves.DO_REFRESH, WBuilder.waveData(MyWaves.ITEM, selectedItem));
```

- `sendWave(waveType, waveData...)` — builds the wave and posts it to JIT for dispatch.
- `WBuilder.waveData(item, value)` — pairs a `WaveItem` key with the payload value.

**2. Using WaveType + WaveBean**

```java
sendWave(MyWaves.DO_REFRESH, myWaveBean);
```

- `sendWave(waveType, waveBean)` — typed bean instead of varargs `WaveData`; good for many fields.


**3. Using a pre-built Wave with WBuilder**

The `WBuilder` fluent API offers a convenient way to create and configure Waves before sending them:

```java
sendWave(WBuilder.callCommand(MyCommand.class));
```

- `WBuilder.callCommand(Class)` — builds a **Call Command** wave ready to send.
- Equivalent to `callCommand(MyCommand.class)` but useful when chaining waves.


How to use — end-to-end
---------------------------

1. Define `WaveType` + `WaveItem` constants in a shared interface.
2. In the receiver: `listen(waveType)` inside `initModel` / `initService` / `initCommand`.
3. Add handler method (`doActionName(...)`) matching items + trailing `Wave`.
4. In the sender: `sendWave(waveType, WBuilder.waveData(item, value))`.
5. Call `unlisten` when the receiver should stop reacting.

Wave Dispatching
-----------------------

When a Wave is sent, the following sequence occurs:

1. The Wave is queued on JIT (JRebirth Internal Thread).
2. The Notifier looks up all components registered for the Wave's `WaveType`.
3. For each registered component, the Notifier resolves the handler method by convention:
   - The WaveType has an **action** name (e.g. `"DO_MY_ACTION"`).
   - The action name is converted to camelCase: `doMyAction`.
   - The Notifier calls this method via reflection, passing the Wave and any typed data items.
4. If a `WaveChecker` is registered for that component + WaveType pair, it is evaluated first — the handler is invoked only when the checker returns `true`.

This convention-over-configuration approach keeps handler wiring completely declarative.


@OnWave Annotation
-----------------------

As an alternative to calling `listen()` manually, you can annotate a method with `@OnWave`.
The framework will auto-register the component for the specified WaveType at startup.

The annotation accepts a single **value** attribute matching the WaveType action string:

```java
@OnWave("MY_ACTION")
protected void myMethod(Wave wave) {
    // handle wave
}
```

- `@OnWave("MY_ACTION")` — auto-registers at startup; method name is free.
- Value must match the `WaveType` action string exactly.


The method name is free — only the annotation value must match the WaveType action.
`@OnWave` is repeatable, so a single method can listen for multiple WaveTypes.

Here is a real example from the Wave showcase:


```java
@Register(value = ModuleModel.class)
public class MainModel extends DefaultFXMLModel<MainModel> implements ModuleModel {

    @OnWave(WWaves.CHANGE)
    protected void whatyouwant(Wave wave) {
        getFXMLController().txtMessage.setText("Wave received : " + wave.waveType().action());
    }

    @Override
    public String moduleName() {
        return "Wave";
    }

    @Override
    public MainController getFXMLController() {
        return (MainController) super.getFXMLController();
    }

}
```

- `@OnWave(WWaves.CHANGE)` — listens without calling `listen()` in `initModel()`.
- Method name (`whatyouwant`) is arbitrary; only the annotation value matters.
- `wave.waveType().action()` — inspect which action was received at runtime.


JRebirth itself uses `@OnWave` internally for built-in UI actions (see *Built-in WaveTypes* below):

```java
@OnWave(WaveConstants.SHOW_VIEW)
void doShowView(final Wave wave);

@OnWave(WaveConstants.HIDE_VIEW)
void doHideView(final Wave wave);
```

- Built into the `Model` interface — every Model handles show/hide automatically.
- Trigger visibility from any component with `WBuilder.showModel(...)` / hide waves.


WaveChecker
-----------------------

A `WaveChecker` lets you filter incoming Waves for a specific component.
Only Waves for which the checker returns `true` are dispatched to the handler method.

`WaveChecker` is a `@FunctionalInterface` extending `Callback<Wave, Boolean>`:


```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface WaveChecker extends Callback<Wave, Boolean> {

}
```

- `WaveChecker` — functional interface: `boolean call(Wave wave)`.
- Return `true` to dispatch to the handler; `false` skips this listener for that wave.


You register a checker with the overloaded `listen` method:


```java
package org.jrebirth.af.api.component.basic;

import java.lang.reflect.Method;

import org.jrebirth.af.api.facade.FacadeReady;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.Wave;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.WaveBean;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.checker.WaveChecker;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveData;
import org.jrebirth.af.api.wave.contract.WaveType;

/**
 * The interface <strong>EnhancedComponent</strong>.
 *
 * Define the contract used to manage waves.
 *
 * @author Sébastien Bordes
 *
 * @param <R> A type that implements FacadeReady
 */
public interface Component<R extends FacadeReady<R>> extends FacadeReady<R> {

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Begin to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * Wave Contract will be checked if {@link org.jrebirth.af.core.resource.provided.JRebirthParameters.DEVELOPER_MODE} parameter is true
     *
     * The wave checker is used to filter the wave if the checker returns false
     *
     * @param waveChecker the wave checker used to forward the wave only if the checker return true
     * @param method the annotated method concerned
     * @param waveType the type(s) to listen
     */
    void listen(final WaveChecker waveChecker, final Method method, final WaveType... waveType);

    /**
     * Stop to listen the type of wave for the current component.
     *
     * @param waveTypes the type(s) to stop to listen
     */
    void unlisten(final WaveType... waveTypes);

    /**
     * Send a wave to the notifier.
     *
     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
