     * The wave will automatically be sent from JRebirthThread.
     *
     * @param waveType the type of wave to send
     * @param waveBean the wave bean
     *
    // ...
```

Example — listen only if the Wave carries a non-null data item:

```java
listen(wave -> wave.contains(MyWaves.ITEM), MyWaves.DO_REFRESH);
```


Threading
-----------------------

By default, wave handler methods run on the thread natural to the component type:

| Component Type | Default Thread |
|----------------|----------------|
| Model          | JAT (JavaFX Application Thread) |
| Service        | JTP (JRebirth Thread Pool) |
| Command        | Depends on command type (JIT, JAT, or JTP) |

You can override the default thread for any handler method with the `@RunInto` annotation:

```java
@RunInto(RunType.JTP)
public void doHeavyComputation(final Wave wave) {
    // runs in the thread pool instead of JAT
}
```

- `@RunInto(JTP)` — override default handler thread for this method only.
- Models default to JAT; Services default to JIT — use when work must move off that thread.


Available `RunType` values: **JAT**, **JAT_SYNC**, **JIT**, **JIT_SYNC**, **JTP**, **JTP_SYNC**, **SAME**.

You can also set a runnable priority:

```java
@RunInto(value = RunType.JTP, priority = PriorityLevel.Highest)
public void doCriticalTask(final Wave wave) { ... }
```

- `priority = PriorityLevel.Highest` — prefer HPTP slot when JTP is busy.
- Combine with `@RunInto(JTP)` for time-sensitive background handlers.


Built-in WaveTypes
-----------------------

JRebirth ships with several built-in WaveTypes defined in `WaveConstants` and used across the framework:

| Constant                        | Purpose |
|---------------------------------|---------|
| `WaveConstants.SHOW_VIEW`       | Show a Model's root node in the scene graph |
| `WaveConstants.HIDE_VIEW`       | Hide a Model's root node from the scene graph |
| `WaveConstants.RETURN_VOID`     | Used internally for void-returning service tasks |

Models automatically listen for `SHOW_VIEW` and `HIDE_VIEW` through the `@OnWave` annotations declared on the `Model` interface, so you can trigger view visibility from any component:

```java
sendWave(WBuilder.showModel(TargetModel.class, myPlaceholder));
```

- `WBuilder.showModel(Class, Node)` — shows the Model's root node inside `myPlaceholder`.
- Uses built-in `SHOW_VIEW` wave; always processed on JAT.


